// hearth — the slope's ember: it lands on something TILTED and runs down it,
// quenching the whole way, so you get a streak rather than a dot.
// `fire.rill`'s ember lands on something flat and stops.
//
// ⚠ "hearth" here means a tilted surface, not a fireplace.
//
// ⚠ Same three manifold axes as `fire.rill` and the same look lives with the
// spray, not here — see that file's header for what a manifold is.
//
//   row.u0  cooled   0 white-hot ... 1 cold
//   row.u1  sooted   0 clean ....... 1 all carbon
//   row.u2  thinned  0 dense ....... 1 blown thin
//
// TWO differences from `fire.rill`, not one:
//
//   1. What tells the kernel it is against the cold thing (below).
//   2. There is no `chill` term. `fire.rill` has a third cooling line —
//      `relax 1 k.chill | mul row.u2`, faster once the ember is thin enough
//      for the air to get at it — and this file does not. A hearth ember
//      therefore cools on two influences where a fire ember cools on three.
//
// A sliding row NEVER sets `row.stuck` — that is what `stuck` means, and a
// running ember is not held. So the condition "I am against a surface right
// now" has nowhere in the row to live: `slide` could write a field, but rill
// lands a row's writes only after the whole node loop, so the lines below
// would read it a tick late, and a value that crosses a tick boundary is
// state owing a dump and a format version. `slate.contact` crosses nothing.
// `slide` says it; these lines read it; the next row starts blank. It is 1 on
// a tick the row is against the surface and 0 otherwise, so `mul
// slate.contact` reads as "this line, but only while touching".
//
// Every `plane.drift.@self.k.<name>` below is a KNOB, not a value: this file
// says what to turn and the number itself is seeded from outside. `drift-run`
// seeds only `gravity`; everything else needs a `--seed` of its own, and a
// knob that never arrives is REFUSED BY NAME at the row rather than read as
// zero. So:
//
//   drift-run --kernel kernels/hearth.rill \\
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.gravity =<cells/s²>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.cool    =<per s, in air>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.plunge  =<per s, on contact>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.smoke   =<per s, carbon in flight>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.quench  =<per s, carbon dumped on landing>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.thin    =<per s, spreading>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.settle  =<per s, pulled back dense>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.puff    =<extra radius when thin>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.grain   =<base radius, cells>
//
// ⚠ A `relax` RATE has a ceiling: a step that would close more than the whole
// gap is refused, so any rate above 1/dt refuses rather than clamping — above
// 60 at 60 Hz, above 10 on a 100 ms headless tick. "Snuff it instantly" is not
// a big number here; it is a refusal.
//
// Units: `row.size` is a RADIUS in cells, `gravity` is cells/s² on y and
// NEGATIVE is down (a positive pull lifts).
spawn
gravity plane.drift.@self.k.gravity
collide | slide
perish

// -- cooled ------------------------------------------------------------
// It cools on its own,
row.u0 | relax 1 plane.drift.@self.k.cool | write row.u0 add
// and far faster wherever it is actually touching the cold thing.
row.u0 | relax 1 plane.drift.@self.k.plunge | mul slate.contact | write row.u0 add

// -- sooted ------------------------------------------------------------
// Carbon as it burns,
row.u1 | relax 1 plane.drift.@self.k.smoke | write row.u1 add
// and a dump of it wherever it is being quenched — which, running down a
// hearth, is the whole length of the streak rather than one mark.
row.u1 | relax 1 plane.drift.@self.k.quench | mul slate.contact | write row.u1 add

// -- thinned -----------------------------------------------------------
// It spreads while it is in the air,
row.u2 | relax 1 plane.drift.@self.k.thin | write row.u2 add
// and is pulled back toward dense while it is against the surface.
row.u2 | relax 0 plane.drift.@self.k.settle | mul slate.contact | write row.u2 add

// The row's size is state, as it is in fire.rill.
row.u2 | mul plane.drift.@self.k.puff | add plane.drift.@self.k.grain | write row.size
